## What Happened

**.NET 11 removed DSA support on macOS.** Apple's Security framework dropped DSA years ago, and .NET on macOS relied on it. Starting in .NET 11, `DSA.Create()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` on macOS (it was already marked `[Obsolete]` in earlier versions).

DSA is considered cryptographically weak and has been deprecated across the ecosystem.

## How to Fix

**Option A — Use BouncyCastle for legacy verification only:**

```csharp
// dotnet add package BouncyCastle.Cryptography
using Org.BouncyCastle.Crypto.Parameters;
using Org.BouncyCastle.Security;

public bool VerifyLegacyDsa(byte[] data, byte[] signature, DSAParameters params)
{
    var p = new DsaParameters(
        new Org.BouncyCastle.Math.BigInteger(1, params.P),
        new Org.BouncyCastle.Math.BigInteger(1, params.Q),
        new Org.BouncyCastle.Math.BigInteger(1, params.G));
    var pubKey = new DsaPublicKeyParameters(
        new Org.BouncyCastle.Math.BigInteger(1, params.Y), p);

    var verifier = SignerUtilities.GetSigner("SHA-1withDSA");
    verifier.Init(false, pubKey);
    verifier.BlockUpdate(data, 0, data.Length);
    return verifier.VerifySignature(signature);
}
```

**Option B — Migrate to a modern algorithm** (preferred long-term):

Replace DSA with **ECDSA** or **Ed25519** for new signatures, and keep BouncyCastle only for verifying old ones during a transition period.

---

**Summary:** The platform removed DSA → .NET followed suit. Use BouncyCastle as a managed (cross-platform) fallback for legacy verification, and plan to migrate off DSA entirely.
